//! USB section of the interface Hardware tab (G1.29b): explanations.
pub(super) const HELP: &[(&str, &str)] = &[
    ("iface_usb.adapter", "The fastest USB the adapter can do: the SuperSpeed capability in its BOS descriptor, else its `bcdUSB`. A USB 3 adapter plugged into a USB 2 port reports 2.10, so the version alone undersells it."),
    ("iface_usb.id", "USB vendor and product ids (`idVendor:idProduct`) and the chip revision (`bcdDevice`). 0bda:8153 rev 3100 is a Realtek RTL8153B."),
    ("iface_usb.over_current", "Times the port cut or limited power because the device drew too much current. Above zero: a bad cable, a weak port or a device that needs a powered hub."),
    ("iface_usb.port", "Where the adapter sits: bus-port (`4-2`: bus 4, port 2; `4-1.3`: port 3 of the hub on port 1), and whether the port itself is USB 3 or USB 2."),
    ("iface_usb.power", "Current the device asks of the port (`bMaxPower`). A USB 2 port gives 500 mA, a USB 3 port 900 mA."),
    ("iface_usb.power_saving", "Autosuspend lets the USB core put an idle device to sleep; LPM (U1/U2) lets a USB 3 link nap between packets. Both are known causes of stalls in USB network adapters: a router keeps them off."),
    ("iface_usb.recoveries", "Recoveries of this adapter since the router started, by level (manual or by the watchdog), and how the last one ended."),
    ("iface_usb.recovery", "## Recovering a stalled USB adapter\n\nThree levels, lightest first:\n\n- **Down/Up:** the interface goes down and up (~1 s); the driver resets the chip.\n- **Restart Driver:** the driver lets go of the adapter and probes it again (~3 s). The network device is recreated; PID 1 gives it its name back and the router puts its configuration back on.\n- **Reset USB Port:** the port is powered off and on when its hub switches power per port, else the device is de-authorized and authorized (the USB core resets it) (~5 s).\n\nOn the port this connection uses, outside Safe Mode, the command asks for `force`."),
    ("iface_usb.section", "## USB adapters in production\n\nA USB Ethernet adapter works, but it is not a PCIe card:\n\n- **one queue, no RSS:** one CPU core does all its work;\n- **latency and jitter** above a PCIe card's (USB moves data in frames of 125 µs);\n- **known stalls:** transmit timeouts, power saving (LPM, autosuspend), a cable that comes loose;\n- **the port caps it:** a gigabit adapter on a USB 2.0 port moves ~300 Mbit/s.\n\nFor WAN and management prefer a PCIe card. The Hardware tab shows the negotiated USB speed and the kernel's transmit timeouts."),
    ("iface_usb.speed", "The USB rate the adapter negotiated with its port: 480 Mbps is USB 2.0, 5 Gbps USB 3.0, 10 Gbps USB 3.1. **Amber** when the port is slower than the adapter: the traffic is capped with no error anywhere. Use a USB 3 port (blue or marked SS), directly, with a USB 3 cable."),
    ("iface_usb.tx_timeouts", "Times the kernel's watchdog (NETDEV WATCHDOG) saw the adapter's transmit queue stop moving. Each one makes the driver reset the adapter (traffic stops for a moment). Growing: the adapter is stalling."),
    ("iface_usb.version", "The USB version the device reports in its descriptor (`bcdUSB`)."),
];
